From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Agent <claude-agent@invalid>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 14:54:30 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] backends/color: Take the target peak from CTA HDR static
 metadata

An output only had a declared target luminance on one route: an
internal panel whose DisplayID 2 extension declares native RGB BT.2020
PQ, in BT.2100 mode. Most HDR displays do not take it. External
monitors and most HDR laptops declare their HDR capability through
CTA-861 HDR static metadata, and their target was always the encoding's
range, so content with a known peak above theirs was never highlight
mapped.

Select the target luminance for every active output in BT.2100 whose
HDR is not forced, from one source as a whole, the first one usable:

 - the DisplayID 2 physical luminance, wherever it is declared, not
   only on the native route, when it declares the 10% window peak; its
   minimum when declared, otherwise the encoding's, never another
   source's; a minimum alone is no target and does not shadow another
   source's peak;
 - the CTA-861 HDR static metadata, its desired content maximum
   luminance as the peak and its desired content minimum luminance as
   black, when it declares a desired maximum; without a desired minimum,
   black stays at the encoding's.

Every source is validated as before, and its peak must also exceed the
encoding's nominal reference white: a peak at or below it is no HDR
target, and mapping highlights toward it would dim ordinary SDR
content. The reference luminance factor does not enter this, so that a
target follows the declarations and not that setting. A source that
fails falls through to the next instead of straight to the fallback.

CTA-861 defines its values as the content range the display considers
optimal, not as a measurement, so they carry their own provenance. KMS
HDR metadata, the encoding and its identity are unchanged.

The native route fixture phases that leave the route now also withdraw
the DisplayID declaration to reach the fallback, and check that leaving
the route alone keeps it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DLZiLBYev72J1s4D37L7Bi
